
{"id":"742c3f0d-74d9-4861-befd-03644ccbe898","object":"chat.completion","created":1784984261,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","reasoning_content":"We need to implement `solve(case)` based on the described task: SE-PE-01. The problem is \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\" for a registered parse tree. Given a payload containing a list of nodes with properties (id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility), and a list of required_symbols. We need to determine which nodes to remove and which to retain, following a set of rules:\n\n- Root cannot be removed.\n- Nodes with required symbols cannot be removed? Actually, \"required_symbols\" is a list of symbols that must remain in the tree after reduction. So any node whose symbol is in required_symbols cannot be removed? But careful: The description says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So if a node's symbol is in required_symbols, it cannot be removed. But note: multiple nodes can have the same symbol? The required_symbols list seems to be a set of symbols that must be present in the tree. So any node with that symbol cannot be removed, because removing it would remove that symbol. Also, nodes that have children that are retained cannot be removed (because you can't remove a parent while keeping children? Actually, removal of a node would remove its subtree. So if you remove a node, all its descendants go away. So to preserve children, the parent must be retained. So \"node with retained children\" means if any descendant is retained, the node cannot be removed. So effectively, removal is permissible only for removable nodes that are not required symbols and whose entire subtree is removed (i.e., no children retained).\n\n- \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So before removing a node, we check that after removal, all required symbols are still present in the tree (i.e., at least one node with that symbol remains). Since required symbols are on nodes, we need to ensure that for each required symbol, at least one node with that symbol is retained.\n\n- \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" So we process nodes by depth descending (deepest first).\n\n- \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So among nodes at the same depth, sort by ID (lexicographically or as given? IDs are strings like \"n1\", \"n2\". Could be compared as strings).\n\n- \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So output should have \"retained_node_ids\" sorted (presumably alphabetically or by ID order?) and \"removed_node_ids\" in the order they are removed? The output schema expects an array for removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids. It says \"removed_node_ids\": array of strings, \"retained_node_ids\": array of strings. Does order matter? The description says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So retained should be sorted, removed should be in the order of removal (the order in which nodes are removed during the process).\n\nWe need to implement an algorithm that traverses nodes from deepest to shallowest, for each node check if it is removable, if its symbol is not required, if removing it would still leave all required symbols present (i.e., there are other nodes with that required symbol elsewhere), and if all other conditions hold (e.g., not root, no retained children). Actually \"no retained children\" is automatically satisfied if we process deepest first: if a node is considered for removal, its children have already been processed (since deeper). If we decide to remove a child, we mark it removed, and then when we consider the parent, we must check whether any of its children are still retained. Since we process from leaves upward, we can track which nodes are retained. Initially, all nodes are considered retained. When we decide to remove a node, we mark it as removed, and also all its descendants become removed. But because we are processing depth descending, and we only remove a node if we haven't removed its children yet? Actually if we remove a node, all descendants are removed as well. So when processing a node, we should only remove it if all its descendants are also being removed (or have already been removed). Since we process depth descending, we will have already considered its children; if we removed some children, then those children are removed; if we retained some children, then those children are retained. So when we get to the parent, we need to ensure that no children are retained. If any child is retained, we cannot remove the parent because that would remove the retained child. So we need to have a set of retained nodes (initially all). When we decide to remove a node, we remove it from retained, and also remove all its descendants from retained (or mark them as removed). But since we process from leaves, we can just check if any of its direct children are still in the retained set; if yes, cannot remove.\n\nThe algorithm:\n\n1. Parse nodes into dict by id: node[id] = {id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility}. Also build parent-child relationships: children[id] = list of children ids.\n\n2. Determine the set of required symbols: required_symbols list.\n\n3. Determine root: node with parent null. Root cannot be removed.\n\n4. Sort nodes by depth descending, then by ID (as tie-breaker) to get processing order.\n\n5. Initialize retained set = all node ids. Initialize removed list = [].\n\n6. For each node in processing order:\n   - Skip root.\n   - Check if node is removable: node['removable'] must be True.\n   - Check if node's symbol is in required_symbols: if yes, skip (cannot remove because required symbol remains only if node is present? Actually if node's symbol is required, but there might be other nodes with same symbol. However the rule says \"Never remove ... a required symbol\". That likely means a node that symbol is in required_symbols cannot be removed, even if others exist. Because \"required symbol\" is ambiguous: it could mean the symbol itself must be preserved, but nodes with that symbol are allowed to be removed if other nodes with that symbol remain. But the phrase \"a required symbol\" suggests the node is a required symbol, i.e., its symbol is required. So we treat such nodes as non-removable. To be safe, let's check: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So \"a required symbol\" likely means a node whose symbol is in required_symbols. So we skip removal for such nodes.\n   - Check if node has any children that are still retained: if yes, skip (cannot remove because would remove retained children).\n   - Check if removing this node would cause any required symbol to be missing: i.e., for each symbol in required_symbols, is there at least one node with that symbol still retained after removal? Since we are tentatively removing, we need to simulate: if we remove this node, we also remove all its descendants (which may include nodes with required symbols). So we need to count the retained nodes per required symbol. We can precompute symbol counts, or check by iterating over all nodes (or just descendants). Since the tree may be small (7 nodes in fixture), we can do a quick check: for each required symbol s, count how many nodes with symbol s are currently in retained set. Then if we remove this node (and its subtree), we subtract from counts the number of nodes with symbol s in the subtree of this node. If any count goes to zero, removal is not allowed.\n   - If all conditions satisfied, then we mark the node for removal:\n        - Remove it from retained set.\n        - Also remove all its descendants from retained set (since they are removed as well). But note: because we process depth descending, descendants might already have been removed or retained. If a descendant is retained, we cannot remove the parent. But we already checked that no children are retained. However, there might be retainable children that we haven't processed yet? No, deeper nodes are processed first, so children (deeper) are processed before parent. So if a child was retained, it means we decided not to remove it when we processed it. So when we get to parent, that child is still retained, so the condition \"node has any children that are still retained\" will fail, and we will not remove parent. So we don't need to re-mark descendants because they are already in retained set. But when we remove parent, we need to also remove all descendants from retained set? Actually, if we remove parent, then all descendants are also removed, even if they were previously retained. But that would be inconsistent because we previously chose to retain a child. However, if we remove parent, we cannot have retained children; so to enforce consistency, we should not have retained children when we remove parent. But if we have already removed some children (they are not in retained set), then removing parent is fine. If a child was retained, we skip parent. So when we remove parent, all its descendants are either already removed (because we removed them when processing them) or are still in retained set because we decided to retain them? But if we retained a child, we cannot remove parent. So the only case where we remove parent is when all children are already removed. Therefore, when we remove parent, we don't need to remove descendants because they are already not in retained set. Except possibly for nodes that are not removable themselves (e.g., required symbols) but are children of a removable parent? But if a child is required symbol, it is non-removable, so it would have been retained; then parent cannot be removed. So again, removal of parent only happens when all children are removable and have been removed (or are not present). So we can just add the parent to removed list and remove from retained set. No need to touch descendants.\n\n7. After processing all nodes, we have retained_set and removed_list (in order of removal). Then sort retained_ids (list of retained_set) by id (probably lexicographically, but could be by insertion order? The requirement: \"Return sorted retained IDs\". We'll sort them as strings ascending).\n\n8. Return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_list, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_sorted}.\n\nBut we must verify with fixture examples. The fixture shows three cases. We can implement and test mentally.\n\nFirst case payload:\n\nNodes:\nid root, parent null, removable false, symbol root (required)\nn1, parent root, depth 1, removable false, symbol s1 (required)\nn2, parent root, depth 1, removable true, symbol s2\nn3, parent root, depth 1, removable false, symbol s0\nn4, parent n2, depth 2, removable true, symbol s1 (required)\nn5, parent n3, depth 2, removable false, symbol s2\nn6, parent n1, depth 2, removable true, symbol s0\n\nRequired symbols: [\"root\", \"s1\"]\n\nProcessing order by depth descending (depth 2 first), then by ID: n4, n5, n6 (depth2), then depth1: n1, n2, n3, then depth0: root.\n\nStart retained: all.\n\n- n4: depth2, removable true, symbol s1 is required -> skip (cannot remove required symbol). So n4 remains retained.\n- n5: depth2, removable false -> cannot remove (removable=False).\n- n6: depth2, removable true, symbol s0 not required. Check children: none (leaf). Check required symbols after removal: we need for each required symbol (root, s1) that at least one node with that symbol remains. Currently, nodes with root: root. nodes with s1: n1 and n4. If we remove n6 and its descendants (none), root and s1 still present. So condition satisfied. Also no retained children. So we can remove n6. Remove from retained. removed_list = [n6]. Retained set becomes all except n6.\n\n- Now depth1: process n1, n2, n3 (order by ID: n1, n2, n3)\n  - n1: depth1, removable false -> skip.\n  - n2: depth1, removable true, symbol s2 not required. Children: n4 (depth2). Is n4 retained? Yes, n4 is still retained (since we skipped it). So n2 has a retained child => cannot remove n2. Skip.\n  - n3: depth1, removable false -> skip.\n\n- root: depth0, removable false -> skip.\n\nFinal retained: root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5. Removed: n6.\n\nBut is that correct? The description might expect a different ordering? Let's think: The objective is \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates while preserving an interestingness oracle.\" The interestingness oracle might be the required_symbols condition. So removal of n6 is allowed because s0 is not required and n6 is removable. n4 is required symbol so cannot remove. n5 is not removable. So result seems plausible.\n\nNow second case: similar structure. We'll test quickly:\nNodes:\nroot (root, depth0, not removable)\nn1 (s1, depth1, not removable)\nn2 (s2, depth1, removable)\nn3 (s0, depth1, not removable)\nn4 (s1, depth2, removable) - but s1 is required, so cannot remove.\nn5 (s2, depth2, not removable)\nn6 (s0, depth2, removable)\nSame as first case essentially. So result should be remove n6 only.\n\nThird case also similar.\n\nBut there might be more complex cases where multiple removable nodes exist and removal may cascade. For example, if a node is removable and its children are also removable and not required, we could remove children first, then parent. The algorithm will remove deepest first, so if a child is removable and not required, it gets removed; then when parent is considered, its children are all removed (since we removed them), so parent becomes eligible for removal (provided its symbol not required and no retained children). So the order of removal would be child first, then parent. That matches \"actual removal order\" which is the order we add them to the list.\n\nEdge cases: if node has multiple children, and some are removable, some not. Then only removable children will be removed if they meet conditions. Those not removable remain retained, so parent cannot be removed.\n\nWhat about nodes that are not removable? They stay.\n\nWe must also handle that required_symbols may have symbols that appear on multiple nodes. If a node with required symbol is removed (if we allowed it), but we disallowed by rule. So fine.\n\nImplementation details: We need to build tree and children mapping. Since parent field is given as id string, we can map.\n\nProcessing order: list nodes sorted by depth descending, then by id string (natural sort? Python default string sort is fine if IDs are consistent like \"n1\", \"n2\", ..., \"n10\". But fixture uses n1..n6, so fine.\n\nWe need to compute for each node its descendant nodes that contain required symbols? For the check of required symbols representation after removal, we can precompute for each node a set of required symbols present in its subtree (or counts). But we can also do a simple check: For each required symbol, we can maintain a count of retained nodes with that symbol. Initially, count = number of nodes with that symbol. Then when we consider removal, we check the subtree's contribution: we can get the number of nodes with that symbol in the subtree (including the node itself). If after removal (subtract from count) any goes to zero, disallow. But we need to know the subtree contribution. Since tree is small, we can compute on the fly: traverse subtree (maybe deep copy of list). But given constraints (standard library only, no recursion depth issues for small trees), we can do a recursive function to collect nodes in subtree. But note: we cannot mutate input. So we can create auxiliary structures.\n\nBetter: Build a list of nodes in order, and also store for each node its descendants (list of ids). Then we can precompute for each node the count per required symbol in its subtree. That would be O(N^2) worst case but N small.\n\nOr we can do: for each required symbol s, store set of node ids that have that symbol. When considering removal of node X, we compute the set of node ids with symbol s in X's subtree (including X). If removal of X would remove all nodes of any required symbol, disallow.\n\nWe can compute subtree node set by traversing from root to leaves and building a descendants set for each node. Since tree is given, we can do: children map; then bottom-up (depth descending) compute set of node ids in subtree. Because depths are given, we can sort by depth descending to compute sets: for leaf, subtree set = {itself}. For internal node, subtree set = {itself} ∪ union of children's subtree sets. This is efficient O(N).\n\nSimilarly, we need to know for each node, which of its descendants are still retained? Actually for the condition \"node with retained children\" we can just check direct children: if any child id is in retained set, then cannot remove. So we need retained set (which changes dynamically). But we are processing in depth descending order, so when we consider a node, its children have already been processed and potentially removed. So we can just check if any child id is in retained set.\n\nThus algorithm:\n\n- Build dict node_map: id -> node.\n- Build children_map: id -> list of child ids.\n- Build depth_sorted list of nodes: sorted by (-depth, id).\n- Compute subtree_ids for each node: {node_id} ∪ union of children's subtree_ids (computed bottom-up from depth descending order ensures children computed before parent).\n- Compute required_symbol_counts: dict symbol -> count of nodes with that symbol.\n\n- Initialize retained_set = set(all_ids)\n- Initialize removed_order = []\n\n- For each node in processing order:\n   if node.id == root: continue\n   if not node['removable']: continue\n   if node['symbol'] in required_symbols: continue   # assuming \"required symbol\" means node's symbol is required\n   # Check if any child retained:\n   children = children_map.get(node.id, [])\n   if any(child in retained_set for child in children): continue\n   # Check required symbols after removal:\n   removed_subtree = subtree_ids[node.id]\n   # For each required symbol, compute how many nodes with that symbol in removed_subtree:\n   # We can precompute for each node the per-symbol count? Or compute on the fly using subtree_ids and node_map.\n   # Since required_symbols is small (2 in fixture), we can compute:\n   violated = False\n   for symbol in required_symbols:\n        count_in_subtree = sum(1 for sub_id in removed_subtree if node_map[sub_id]['symbol'] == symbol)\n        if required_symbol_counts[symbol] - count_in_subtree == 0:\n            violated = True\n            break\n   if violated: continue\n   # Remove node:\n   retained_set.discard(node.id)\n   # Also remove all descendants? Not needed because we already checked no retained children, and descendants are either already removed or not? Actually, if a descendant was not removable (required) it would have been retained, and then we would not be able to remove parent because that descendant is a child? Not necessarily direct child. But if a descendant is retained and is not a direct child, the direct child that is its ancestor would also be retained? Actually, if a descendant is retained, all its ancestors up to parent must be retained as well? Not necessarily: if we have root -> A -> B, and we decided to remove A? But we cannot remove A while B is retained because B is a child of A (direct descendant), so our check would catch that. So if we remove a node, all its descendants are effectively removed because we remove the node itself, and we don't need to explicitly remove descendants because we already know that none are retained (since any retained descendant would have a retained path up to the direct children, which we blocked). However, if a descendant was already removed during its own processing, then it's not in retained_set. So we are safe. But to be thorough, we can also remove all descendants from retained_set just in case there are descendants that are still retained but we missed? But we already verified no direct child is retained, and since the tree is hierarchical, if no direct child is retained, then all deeper descendants are also not retained because they would have been removed when their parent (the child) was processed, unless the child was not removed because it was required? But if child is required, it is retained, so direct child retained -> cannot remove parent. So it works.\n\n   removed_order.append(node.id)\n   # Update required_symbol_counts? Not necessary for future checks because we will not check removal for nodes that are ancestors that might rely on this? Actually, when we remove a node, the counts of required symbols in the whole tree decrease. Subsequent ancestors might be checked for removal, and they need the updated counts. But we can just compute the count after removal by subtracting the subtree contribution. Since we already compute on the fly for each node, we can use the original total counts and subtract the subtree contribution each time. But if we remove multiple nodes, the total count should reflect the removals. However, if we remove a node, its subtree is gone. If later we consider an ancestor, its subtree includes the removed node? No, because the node is removed, it should not be considered as part of the ancestor's subtree when checking removal of ancestor? Actually, when we consider removing an ancestor, we want to know the effect of removing that ancestor. If we already removed some descendants, those descendants are no longer in the tree, so they should not be counted. The subtree_ids we precomputed include all original descendants, regardless of removal. That is a problem. Because if we previously removed a child, then when we consider removing the parent, we should not double-count that child as being removed again. But our check for \"retained children\" ensures we don't remove parent if any child is retained. So if we removed a child, that child is not retained, so we can remove parent. But then the subtree of the parent includes that child, so when we compute counts of required symbols in the parent's subtree, we would include that child's symbol type. However, that child is already gone, so the actual impact of removing the parent on required symbols is only from the descendants that are still present (i.e., those that are retained). The precomputed subtree_ids are static; they include all original nodes, even those already removed. This would overestimate the removal impact and could cause us to incorrectly reject removal of a parent because we think removing it would remove a required symbol that is already gone. So we need to either update subtree sets dynamically, or compute the count on the fly using only retained nodes. Since we process top-down (or bottom-up) and removal decisions are made, we can maintain a set of retained nodes. Then for each candidate removal, we can compute the actual set of nodes that would be removed: the node itself plus all its descendants that are still retained. Because if a descendant is already removed, it cannot be removed again. So we need to get the current retained subtree of the node. This can be computed by traversing the retained_set downward from the node, following children that are in retained_set. Since the tree is small, we can do a BFS/DFS on retained_set.\n\nSimplify: At each candidate removal, we collect all nodes in the retained subtree (node + all descendants that are in retained_set). Then check if any of those have a required symbol, and for each required symbol, if the total count of retained nodes with that symbol minus the number in this retained subtree would become zero. If not, then we remove the entire block (node and its retained descendant subtree). Remove them from retained_set and add them to removed_order (maybe we need to add the node first? The removal order should list the node itself first, then its descendants? The description says \"actual removal order\". When we remove a parent, we are effectively removing the whole subtree at once. But does the order matter? Possibly we should list the parent first, then descendants? Or list them in the order they are removed during the processing? Since we process deepest first, if we remove a parent, its children have already been processed and either removed or retained. If we remove parent, we must also remove any retained children that we previously decided to retain? That seems inconsistent. Because we cannot retain a child and then remove parent. So the only scenario where we remove parent is when all children are already removed. So the retained subtree of a parent at the time of its removal is just the parent itself (since children are already gone). So no need to descend further. So our earlier logic of checking only direct children for retention and not needing to cascade removal of descendants is correct as long as we process depth descending and only remove nodes when all children are gone. This ensures that when we remove a node, its entire subtree is already removed (because any child that was removable and not required would have been removed earlier, and any child that is non-removable or required would have prevented removal). So the retained_subtree of a node at the time of its removal is just the node itself. Therefore, we don't need to dynamically compute subtree sets; we can rely on the fact that we only remove leaves of the retained tree (after processing all deeper nodes). So the static subtree_ids can be used for the purpose of checking required symbols, but we need to be careful: if a node was removed earlier, its descendants are no longer present. For an ancestor, the subtree_ids includes those removed descendants, which would falsely indicate that removing the ancestor would remove required symbols that are already gone. So we should ignore the removed descendants. To avoid complexity, we can simply not use subtree_ids; instead, for each candidate removal, we check if any of its descendants in the retained set contain a required symbol. But since we are removing nodes only when all its children are already removed, the only descendants possibly retained are those that are non-removable or required symbols. But if such a descendant existed, the parent would have a retained child, and removal would be disallowed. So the parent's entire subtree is already removed, except possibly the parent itself. Therefore, when we consider removing the parent, there are no retained descendants. So the check for required symbols reduces to: is the parent's symbol required? And the other required symbols are already present elsewhere (since deeper nodes with required symbols have been retained and cannot be removed). So we can just check if the node's symbol is required (already handled) and that for each required symbol, there is at least one retained node with that symbol. Since we only remove the parent itself, we need to ensure that for each required symbol, there is at least one retained node with that symbol (including the parent if its symbol is required, but we already excluded that). So we can simply maintain a count of retained nodes per required symbol. When we initially compute counts, they include all nodes. When we remove a node, we decrement the count for its symbol. For a candidate removal, we check if after decrementing, any symbol count becomes zero. If yes, skip. This is simpler and correct because we only remove the node itself (since its children are already gone). But what if a node has children that are not removable and not required? For example, a node with a child that is not removable and not required (because removable=False, symbol not required). That child would be retained because we cannot remove it (removable false). So the parent would have a retained child, so we cannot remove the parent. So the parent's subtree still has that child, and if we attempted to remove the parent, we would be removing that child as well. But we already prevent that because of the retained child check. So no issue.\n\nThus the simplified algorithm:\n\n- Build node map\n- Build children map\n- required_symbols set\n- required_symbol_counts: dict symbol -> initial count from all nodes\n- processing order list of node ids sorted by (-depth, id)\n- retained_set = set(all_ids)\n- removed_order = []\n\n- For each node_id in processing order:\n    node = node_map[node_id]\n    if node_id == root_id: continue\n    if not node['removable']: continue\n    if node['symbol'] in required_symbols: continue\n    # Check retained children\n    children = children_map.get(node_id, [])\n    if any(child in retained_set for child in children): continue\n    # Check required symbols after removal\n    symbol = node['symbol']\n    # We need to check if after removing this node, any required symbol count goes to zero.\n    # Since we only remove this node, we check if required_symbol_counts[req_sym] - (1 if req_sym == symbol else 0) becomes zero for any req_sym.\n    # Actually we need to consider all required symbols. The only symbol that changes count is the node's symbol. But if node's symbol is not required, no change. So we only need to check for required symbols that might become zero if we remove a node that has a required symbol? But we already skip nodes with required symbols. So this check might be redundant. However, consider a required symbol that appears only on nodes that are not removable? That's fine. But the condition \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So we need to ensure that after removal, for each required symbol, there is still at least one node with that symbol in the retained set. Since we only remove this node, and its symbol is not required, the counts of required symbols are unchanged. So condition always holds. But there is a nuance: what if a node's removal would cause its ancestors to become root? No. So perhaps the condition is automatically satisfied for removable non-required nodes. But what about the case where a required symbol appears only on nodes that are deep and removable? But we already prevented removal of nodes with required symbols. So yes, condition is automatically satisfied. However, the description says \"Never remove ... a required symbol\" – so we must not remove nodes with required symbols. So we can skip the condition. But we should still include a safety check.\n\nThus we can skip the required symbols check for non-required nodes, but for robustness, we can still check: after removal, for each req_sym, required_symbol_counts[req_sym] - (1 if node['symbol'] == req_sym else 0) > 0. Since node['symbol'] not required, always true.\n\nSo algorithm:\n\nif any retained child: continue\nelse: remove node, add to removed_order, remove from retained_set, decrement required_symbol_counts[node['symbol']] (if needed for future checks? But not needed because we will not remove further nodes that depend on this count? Actually, if later we consider removal of an ancestor, we need updated counts. For example, suppose a required symbol appears on a node that we removed? That cannot happen because we don't remove required symbol nodes. So counts remain unchanged except for non-required symbols. But non-required symbols counts don't matter for the check. So we can ignore updates. But to be safe, we can update counts for non-required symbols as well; it doesn't hurt.\n\nBut there is a scenario: suppose a required symbol s appears on two nodes: one that is removable and one that is not? But we cannot remove the removable one because it's required? Actually we said we cannot remove nodes with required symbols, so we never touch them. So counts stay same.\n\nThus the algorithm is simple.\n\nLet's test on first fixture:\n\nInitial required_symbol_counts: root:1, s1:2 (n1 and n4). Other symbols not required.\n\nProcessing order: n4 (depth2), n5 (depth2), n6 (depth2), n1 (d1), n2 (d1), n3 (d1), root.\n\nn4: removable true but symbol s1 required -> skip.\nn5: removable false -> skip.\nn6: removable true, symbol s0 not required, children none, no retained children? Actually children none. Check retained children: none. So remove. removed_order = [n6], retained_set remove n6.\nn1: removable false -> skip.\nn2: removable true, children: n4 (retained), so retained child -> cannot remove. Skip.\nn3: removable false -> skip.\nroot: skip.\n\nFinal retained: all except n6. removed: n6. That matches previous.\n\nSecond case: same.\n\nThird case: same.\n\nThus the solution seems straightforward.\n\nPotential edge cases: if there are nodes with removable true and no children, and symbol not required, they will be removed if they are deepest and no retained children. That's correct.\n\nWhat about nodes that have children that are removed earlier, and parent is removable and not required? Then after children are removed, parent becomes a leaf in the retained set, and can be removed. That would be removed in order: child first, then parent.\n\nExample: root (not removable) -> A (removable, symbol not required) -> B (removable, symbol not required). Processing order: B first (depth2), removable, no children, no required symbol, check children? none, so remove B. Then A (depth1): children = [B], but B is no longer in retained_set, so no retained children, and A is removable, symbol not required, so remove A. So removed_order = [B, A]. That seems correct.\n\nNow, what about nodes with multiple children where some children are removed and some are not? Then parent cannot be removed because it still has retained children.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\n1. Parse case: case is a dict with keys \"nodes\" and \"required_symbols\". There may be \"_case_nonce\" but ignore.\n\n2. Build node_map from nodes list. Ensure each node has required fields: id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. We can validate.\n\n3. Identify root_id: node with parent None or null? In fixtures, root parent is null. So check parent is None.\n\n4. Bu